.TH FORT 1 "August 2026" "Fort" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
fort \- securely bootstrap, harden, audit, and verify Linux servers

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fort
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ command-options ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Fort
is a Linux server security bootstrap and hardening utility designed for
newly provisioned VPS, cloud, and dedicated servers.

Fort automates common host-security tasks including SSH hardening,
administrator provisioning, firewall configuration, brute-force
protection, automatic security updates, kernel hardening, auditing,
and post-hardening verification.

Fort is designed to provide a secure baseline while minimizing the risk
of administrator lockout.

The primary target platforms are Debian and Ubuntu servers.

.SH COMMANDS

.TP
.B fort harden
Apply the Fort security baseline to the current server.

This may configure SSH, firewall rules, Fail2Ban, automatic updates,
kernel security settings, audit logging, and other supported controls.

.TP
.B fort audit
Inspect the current server configuration without modifying it.

Reports detected security weaknesses, exposed services, unsafe SSH
configuration, firewall state, update configuration, and other host
security findings.

.TP
.B fort verify
Verify that the expected Fort security controls are active.

Typical checks include:

.RS
.IP \(bu 2
root SSH login disabled
.IP \(bu 2
password-based SSH authentication disabled
.IP \(bu 2
public-key authentication enabled
.IP \(bu 2
administrator account available
.IP \(bu 2
SSH configuration valid
.IP \(bu 2
firewall active
.IP \(bu 2
default inbound firewall policy restricted
.IP \(bu 2
Fail2Ban active
.IP \(bu 2
automatic security updates enabled
.IP \(bu 2
auditd active
.IP \(bu 2
kernel hardening settings loaded
.IP \(bu 2
unexpected listening services detected
.RE

.TP
.B fort status
Display a concise summary of the server security state.

.TP
.B fort doctor
Perform diagnostic checks for Fort itself and the underlying operating
system.

This command can be used to detect unsupported environments, missing
packages, invalid configuration, or services that prevent Fort from
operating correctly.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display command help.

.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display the installed Fort version.

.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable additional diagnostic output.

.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress non-essential output.

.TP
.BR \-\-dry\-run
Show intended changes without applying them.

.TP
.BR \-\-yes
Automatically accept supported non-critical confirmation prompts.

Use this option carefully on remote systems.

.SH HARDEN OPTIONS

The following options may be available with
.BR "fort harden" .

.TP
.BI \-\-admin " USER"
Set the administrative Linux account that should retain SSH and sudo
access.

Example:

.RS
.nf
fort harden --admin mahros
.fi
.RE

.TP
.BI \-\-ssh\-key " KEY"
Install the specified SSH public key for the administrator.

An Ed25519 key is recommended for normal deployments.

.TP
.BI \-\-ssh\-key\-file " FILE"
Read the administrator SSH public key from
.IR FILE .

Example:

.RS
.nf
fort harden --admin mahros \\
    --ssh-key-file ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
.fi
.RE

.TP
.BI \-\-ssh\-port " PORT"
Configure the SSH daemon to listen on
.IR PORT .

The default SSH port is 22.

Changing the SSH port should not be treated as a replacement for strong
authentication.

.TP
.B \-\-allow\-http
Permit inbound TCP port 80 through the host firewall.

.TP
.B \-\-allow\-https
Permit inbound TCP port 443 through the host firewall.

.TP
.B \-\-no\-http
Do not permit inbound HTTP traffic.

.TP
.B \-\-no\-https
Do not permit inbound HTTPS traffic.

.TP
.B \-\-allow\-ssh\-forwarding
Permit SSH TCP forwarding.

This should only be enabled when SSH tunnels are required.

.TP
.B \-\-allow\-agent\-forwarding
Permit SSH agent forwarding.

Agent forwarding should remain disabled unless explicitly required.

.SH SSH HARDENING

Fort may apply the following OpenSSH security controls:

.RS
.IP \(bu 2
disable remote root login
.IP \(bu 2
disable password authentication
.IP \(bu 2
disable keyboard-interactive authentication
.IP \(bu 2
require public-key authentication
.IP \(bu 2
reject empty passwords
.IP \(bu 2
restrict SSH access to authorized administrator accounts
.IP \(bu 2
reduce permitted authentication attempts
.IP \(bu 2
reduce login grace time
.IP \(bu 2
disable X11 forwarding
.IP \(bu 2
disable SSH tunneling where not explicitly required
.IP \(bu 2
disable SSH agent forwarding where not explicitly required
.IP \(bu 2
increase SSH security logging
.RE

Before activating a modified SSH configuration, Fort should validate the
configuration using the installed SSH daemon.

Fort should not intentionally reload an invalid SSH configuration.

.SH FIREWALL

Fort uses the native Linux packet filtering facilities supported by the
target operating system.

On supported Debian systems, nftables is preferred.

The default server policy is intended to follow a deny-by-default model
for unsolicited inbound traffic while allowing established connections
and explicitly permitted services.

A typical web server configuration exposes only:

.RS
.nf
22/tcp     SSH
80/tcp     HTTP
443/tcp    HTTPS
.fi
.RE

Applications such as PostgreSQL, Redis, MinIO, administrative panels,
and internal application ports should normally not be exposed directly
to the public Internet.

.SH DOCKER

Fort does not assume that host firewall rules alone completely define
the attack surface of Docker workloads.

Container runtimes may create their own packet-filtering and port
forwarding rules.

Operators should inspect Docker port publishing separately.

For internal-only services, avoid configurations such as:

.RS
.nf
ports:
  - "5432:5432"
.fi
.RE

when PostgreSQL is not intended to be publicly accessible.

Where host access is required but public access is not, binding to
loopback may be appropriate:

.RS
.nf
ports:
  - "127.0.0.1:5432:5432"
.fi
.RE

.SH FAIL2BAN

Fort may configure Fail2Ban to detect repeated SSH authentication
failures and temporarily block abusive sources.

Fail2Ban is considered an additional defense and not a replacement for
public-key authentication or firewall controls.

.SH AUTOMATIC UPDATES

Fort may configure unattended security updates using the operating
system package manager.

On Debian and Ubuntu, this normally uses
.BR unattended-upgrades .

Operators should still monitor reboot requirements and application
compatibility after security updates.

.SH KERNEL HARDENING

Fort applies conservative kernel and network security settings intended
to reduce unnecessary attack surface without intentionally breaking
normal VPS workloads.

Controls may include:

.RS
.IP \(bu 2
disable ICMP redirects
.IP \(bu 2
disable source-routed packets
.IP \(bu 2
enable TCP SYN cookies
.IP \(bu 2
restrict kernel pointer exposure
.IP \(bu 2
restrict unprivileged access to kernel logs
.IP \(bu 2
restrict ptrace behavior
.IP \(bu 2
enable protected symbolic-link and hard-link behavior
.RE

Fort intentionally avoids blindly applying every available sysctl
hardening recommendation.

Some settings may conflict with container networking, routing,
virtualization, debugging, or application requirements.

.SH AUDITING

Fort may install and enable the Linux Audit subsystem using
.BR auditd .

Fort can monitor changes to security-sensitive resources including:

.RS
.IP \(bu 2
/etc/passwd
.IP \(bu 2
/etc/group
.IP \(bu 2
/etc/shadow
.IP \(bu 2
/etc/sudoers
.IP \(bu 2
/etc/sudoers.d/
.IP \(bu 2
/etc/ssh/
.IP \(bu 2
system network configuration
.IP \(bu 2
scheduled-task configuration
.RE

.SH BACKUPS

Before replacing supported configuration files, Fort should create
backup copies where practical.

Backups must be protected from unprivileged users.

The exact backup location may vary by Fort version.

.SH LOCKOUT PROTECTION

Fort is intended to avoid common remote-server lockout conditions.

Before disabling root or password-based SSH access, Fort should verify
that:

.RS
.IP 1. 3
the administrative account exists;
.IP 2. 3
the account has an authorized SSH public key;
.IP 3. 3
the SSH configuration is syntactically valid;
.IP 4. 3
the SSH port remains permitted through the firewall.
.RE

Even with these protections, administrators are strongly advised to
keep their existing SSH session open while testing a second SSH login.

Example:

.RS
.nf
ssh mahros@203.0.113.10
.fi
.RE

After login, verify privilege escalation:

.RS
.nf
sudo whoami
.fi
.RE

Expected output:

.RS
.nf
root
.fi
.RE

Only after successful verification should the original administrative
session be closed.

.SH ENVIRONMENT

Fort may recognize the following environment variables.

.TP
.B ADMIN_USER
Default administrator account.

.TP
.B SSH_PUBLIC_KEY
Public key to install for the administrator.

.TP
.B SSH_PORT
SSH listening port.

.TP
.B ALLOW_HTTP
Permit inbound HTTP when set to a supported true value.

.TP
.B ALLOW_HTTPS
Permit inbound HTTPS when set to a supported true value.

.TP
.B ALLOW_SSH_TCP_FORWARDING
Control SSH TCP forwarding.

.TP
.B ALLOW_SSH_AGENT_FORWARDING
Control SSH agent forwarding.

Command-line options should take precedence over equivalent environment
variables.

.SH EXAMPLES

Create a secure administrator and harden a new server:

.RS
.nf
fort harden \\
    --admin mahros \\
    --ssh-key-file ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
.fi
.RE

.PP
Harden a web server:

.RS
.nf
fort harden \\
    --admin mahros \\
    --ssh-key-file ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub \\
    --allow-http \\
    --allow-https
.fi
.RE

.PP
Inspect a server without changing it:

.RS
.nf
fort audit
.fi
.RE

.PP
Verify security controls:

.RS
.nf
fort verify
.fi
.RE

.PP
Display current security state:

.RS
.nf
fort status
.fi
.RE

.PP
Preview changes:

.RS
.nf
fort harden --dry-run
.fi
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Operation completed successfully.

.TP
.B 1
General error or failed security check.

.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage.

.TP
.B 3
Unsupported operating system or environment.

.TP
.B 4
Security configuration validation failed.

.TP
.B 5
Operation aborted to prevent possible administrator lockout.

Additional exit codes may be introduced in future releases.

.SH FILES

.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config
Primary OpenSSH server configuration.

.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/
OpenSSH configuration fragments.

.TP
.I /etc/nftables.conf
Primary nftables configuration on supported systems.

.TP
.I /etc/fail2ban/
Fail2Ban configuration.

.TP
.I /etc/sysctl.d/
Persistent kernel parameter configuration.

.TP
.I /etc/audit/
Linux Audit configuration.

.TP
.I /var/log/journal/
Persistent systemd journal storage when enabled.

.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS

Fort provides a security baseline, not an absolute security guarantee.

Host hardening does not replace:

.RS
.IP \(bu 2
application security
.IP \(bu 2
dependency management
.IP \(bu 2
container security
.IP \(bu 2
secret management
.IP \(bu 2
network architecture
.IP \(bu 2
off-site backups
.IP \(bu 2
monitoring and incident response
.IP \(bu 2
regular vulnerability management
.RE

Operators should expose only services that are explicitly required.

Administrative services and databases should normally remain on private
networks, container networks, loopback interfaces, or authenticated
VPNs.

.SH NOTES

Fort favors security controls with a favorable security-to-compatibility
tradeoff.

It intentionally avoids applying obscure or highly disruptive hardening
settings merely to increase the number of enabled controls.

The desired result is a hardened server that remains operational,
observable, maintainable, and recoverable.

.SH BUGS

Report security-sensitive vulnerabilities privately rather than through
public issue trackers when a private reporting mechanism is available.

